course: dictionary
lesson: 4319
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it, which is its context. «The grouse flew up from the heather.» There are feathers, heather and flying here, so grouse must be a bird.
explain: «Dad began to grouse about the wet socks.» Now grouse is a verb, and it means to complain or grumble. The clue is the word about, and the wet socks are what he is moaning about. A grouse can also be the complaint itself.
explain: Some words travel and change meaning. In Australia, «that's grouse!» means «that's great!», so your Aussie friend is pleased, not cross. That use is a bit old-fashioned now, so check a dictionary when you are unsure, and always try your meaning in the sentence.
fact: A grouse is a plump, brown wild bird with feathered legs that lives on moors and in forests.
fact: Red grouse live on heather moors in Scotland and northern England, and North America has ruffed grouse and sage grouse.
fact: «The capercaillie, hlukhar or "the deaf one" in Ukrainian, is the biggest grouse.» It is now rare.
fact: During its clicking spring song, the capercaillie seems for a moment not to hear, which is why it is called "the deaf one".
fact: To grouse means to complain or grumble, and a grouse is a complaint.
exercise: «The grouse burst out of the heather with a loud call.» What is a grouse here? | *A plump, brown wild bird | A very good day | A complaint | A kind of flower
exercise: «Mum began to grouse about the wet socks.» What does grouse mean here? | *To complain or grumble | To feel very happy | To count the socks | To fly up quickly
exercise: An Aussie friend says, «That's grouse!» after a great goal. What does she mean? | That's a complaint! | That's a bird! | That's too wet! | *That's great!
test: «Her grouse was that the bus was always late.» What is a grouse in this sentence? | Something very good | A type of bus | A wild bird | *A complaint
test: Where do grouse live, according to this lesson? | Under the ground | In busy cities | *On moors and in forests | Only in the sea
exam: «Ella does nothing but grouse all day.» Which meaning of grouse fits this sentence best? | Really good or excellent | *To complain or grumble | A plump brown bird | To fly up from the heather
